<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Taygetus</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Taygetos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Taygetus is the highest mountain range of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, rising to 2,404 metres at Profitis Ilias. It runs north to south between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laconia">Laconia</a> and Messenia and towers over the site of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> to the west. The range was the barrier that Spartan armies crossed to conquer Messenia, and later writers told how Spartans exposed weak infants in a chasm on its slopes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taygetus.jpg" alt="Taygetus" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Herbert Ortner, Vienna, Austria · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Taygetos_Ilias_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
